\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.1}{\ignorespaces ROI Projection Under Missing Signal Integration. Conservative scenario (solid blue) assumes linear additivity of feature improvements (+9.8\% lift from baseline). Realistic scenario (dashed blue) accounts for 20\% correlation between feature categories (+8.4\% lift). Current baseline (-7.5\% ROI, red dashed) reflects empirical performance. Full integration reaches marginally profitable range (+0.9\% to +2.3\% ROI) but falls short of original +5\% target (green dash-dot).}}{248}{figure.caption.1624}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.2}{\ignorespaces Comparison of 90\% credible interval coverage across all methods investigated. The horizontal dashed line indicates the target coverage of 90\%. All single-output BNN variants (baseline, with Vegas features, with opponent features, with optimized hyperparameters) remain severely under-calibrated regardless of modifications. In contrast, non-Bayesian baselines (conformal prediction, quantile regression) and the architectural innovation (multi-output BNN) achieve calibration near or exceeding the target.}}{252}{figure.caption.1641}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.3}{\ignorespaces Incremental impact of feature engineering on 90\% credible interval coverage. Each bar represents a cumulative feature configuration: baseline (4 features), +Vegas lines (6 features), +environment (10 features), +opponent defense (9 features, environment features removed). Error bars represent bootstrapped 95\% confidence intervals (1000 resamples). The horizontal red dashed line indicates the target 90\% coverage. Despite incorporating domain expertise and multiple information sources, the best feature set improves coverage by only 5.1 percentage points, leaving the model severely under-calibrated.}}{255}{figure.caption.1650}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.4}{\ignorespaces Coverage-sharpness trade-off across uncertainty quantification methods. Each point represents a method, with x-axis showing average 90\% interval width (lower is sharper) and y-axis showing empirical coverage (closer to 90\% is better calibrated). The dashed horizontal line indicates target 90\% coverage; the shaded region marks acceptable calibration (85-95\%). The dashed vertical line shows the single-output BNN's interval width. Single-output BNN variants cluster in the bottom-left (narrow but under-calibrated), while quantile regression and conformal prediction achieve calibration by drastically widening intervals. The multi-output BNN achieves near-optimal balance: strong calibration without extreme interval widening.}}{263}{figure.caption.1695}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.5}{\ignorespaces BNN calibration reliability diagram comparing predicted probabilities to observed frequencies across different confidence levels. The multi-output BNN (blue line) closely tracks the perfect calibration diagonal, while the single-output BNN baseline (orange line) shows systematic under-calibration. Each point represents a binned confidence level, with error bars showing 95\% confidence intervals from bootstrapping.}}{265}{figure.caption.1699}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.6}{\ignorespaces BNN Calibration Improvement Progression}}{270}{figure.caption.1718}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.7}{\ignorespaces BNN Improvement Hierarchy}}{273}{figure.caption.1730}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.8}{\ignorespaces Evolution of ensemble performance across model versions, showing progression in ROI, Win Rate, and Sharpe Ratio from baseline v1.0 (3-model ensemble with 9 features) to 5-Way v3.1 (5-model ensemble with 157 features). Each metric shows consistent improvement, with the 5-way ensemble achieving 6.8\% ROI, 61\% win rate, and 2.6 Sharpe ratio.}}{279}{figure.caption.1778}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.9}{\ignorespaces Temporal evolution of cumulative ROI across 2022--2024 backtest period for all ensemble versions. The 5-way ensemble (v3.1) demonstrates consistent upward trajectory with minimal drawdown periods, achieving 6.8\% cumulative ROI. Shaded regions indicate 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals.}}{280}{figure.caption.1782}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.10}{\ignorespaces Training window ablation study results comparing four scenarios on the 2025 hold-out set. Scenario B (2018--2024 with H=4 exponential decay on 2015--2017) achieves the best MAE (47.77 yards) and is statistically superior to both strict 2018--2024 ($p=0.003$) and current 2020--2024 ($p=0.007$) windows. Error bars represent 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals.}}{284}{figure.caption.1801}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.11}{\ignorespaces Coverage-sharpness trade-off for uncertainty quantification methods. Each point represents a method, with x-axis showing average 90\% interval width (lower is sharper) and y-axis showing empirical coverage on 2024 holdout (closer to 90\% is better calibrated). The shaded region marks acceptable calibration (85--95\%). Temperature scaling achieves near-optimal balance: strong calibration (89.5\%) with sharp intervals (17 yards). Conformal methods guarantee coverage at the cost of 4-fold wider intervals.}}{299}{figure.caption.1927}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.12}{\ignorespaces Coverage stability over time (2024 season, weeks 1--18). Conformal prediction maintains near-constant coverage (90--92\%) across all weeks, validating distribution-free guarantees under mild covariate shift. Temperature scaling exhibits slight drift (87--91\%), reflecting sensitivity to distributional changes not captured by fixed $T=0.75$.}}{300}{figure.caption.1934}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.13}{\ignorespaces Evidential regression calibration curve (left) and normalized residual plot (right). The calibration curve reveals severe over-calibration: all predictions fall within even narrow confidence intervals. This indicates the model is too conservative with uncertainty estimates.}}{311}{figure.caption.2010}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.14}{\ignorespaces Uncertainty decomposition for evidential regression. Top left: Aleatoric uncertainty (irreducible data noise, mean 2,359 yards). Top right: Epistemic uncertainty (model uncertainty, mean 11,391 yards). Bottom left: Scatter plot shows epistemic dominates aleatoric. Bottom right: Total uncertainty distribution (mean 11,638 yards).}}{313}{figure.caption.2018}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.15}{\ignorespaces GNN Calibration Plot (2025 Test Set): Predicted probabilities vs observed frequencies. Diagonal line indicates perfect calibration. Our GNN (5 Message Rounds) shows slight overconfidence in extreme predictions ($p < 0.3$ or $p > 0.7$) but maintains reasonable calibration overall.}}{318}{figure.caption.2058}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.16}{\ignorespaces Causal DAG for NFL Rushing Performance. Solid arrows represent causal relationships. Dashed red paths show confounding backdoor paths from treatment (carries) to outcome (yards) that must be blocked via adjustment. Minimal adjustment set: \{Player Ability, Team Quality, Opponent Defense\}. Game Script is a collider (affected by Team Quality) and should NOT be conditioned on to avoid inducing bias.}}{324}{figure.caption.2098}%
